Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.270 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to those of you who are visiting  today it's great to have you here we're  between that in-between time the week  after exams and the week before  commencement it will look very different  there will be 15,000 white chairs out  there on old campus and there will be  lots of people so go and have lunch  today while the restaurants are empty  it's a pleasure to welcome you here  today and I had planned a sermon that  was going to be very easy but the  scriptures got in the way this would be  a great sunday to devote to the Good  Shepherd this is Good Shepherd sunday we  hear about the Good Shepherd God as the  Good Shepherd in the psalm in those  timeless words that say the Lord is my  shepherd I shall not want we hear of  that God again and first Peter where  Jesus is referred to as the Shepherd and  guardian of your souls we hear of him  again in the Gospel as the gate for the  Sheep as annabelle just read where it  says whoever enters by me will be kept  safe and will come in and go out and  find pasture this would be a good sunday  to spend with that good shepherd we're  all little tired after the semester but  there's a problem in our scriptures  today which as much as I want it to  smooth over it and move on to this word  of comfort as much as I wanted to spend  today in those green pastures beside  those still waters I just couldn't  because there was something I felt I had  to address the problem is 1st Peter  chapter 2 verse 18 if you look in your  worship bulletin you'll notice that our  reading today is first Peter 2 verses 19  to 25 Beverly Stern's read the passage  beautifully and it is in many ways a  beautiful message a message about  enduring suffering first Peter 219 says  it is a credit to you if being aware of  God you endure pain while suffering on  lastly what a word of encouragement for  those who are suffering unjustly but i'm  afraid the church has used this passage  in bad ways over the years i might argue  as to how far we can take this idea of  quiet endurance of unjust suffering but  i think i could work with it it speaks  to courage it speaks to peacemaking it  speaks to hope but the preceding verse  the verse left out of our reading is the  problem and the fact that it's left out  of our reading is a problem for it says  slaves accept the authority of your  masters with all deference not only  those who are kind and gentle but also  those who are harsh this is the problem  the problem is our scriptures our  beloved scriptures the word we pray  should be a lamp to our feet and a light  to our path is saying something we just  can't accept and saying something we  cannot wish or wash away I'd feel better  if I could preach to you about the Good  Shepherd today I'd feel better if I  could say to you yea though I walk  through the valley of the shadow of  death I will fear no evil for thou art  with me thy rod and thy staff they  comfort me but first Peter 218 colors  even these words hearing my Scripture  seem to teach to Christian slaves that  they accept patiently and silently the  abuse the abuses of their masters as  though that was right seems to change  even Psalm 23 now thy rod and thy staff  sound very different imagining the rod  used on another human being how can I  imagine the valley of the shadow of  death without seeing all those slaves  there who died there the scripture is a  problem and digging deeper into its  history this problem only gets worse  because in the history of our own nation  these very vs first Peter two were used  regularly  to justify slavery as God ordained the  way of nature and order and peaceful  society and not only for slaves but  first Peter 2 has been used often to  send battered wives back to their  battered battering husbands with the  instruction to endure silently just as  Christ endured the cross in fact for  anyone suffering in an unjust order bday  slaves or women african-americans or  simply the poor this particular  scripture first Peter 2 and others like  it have been presented by that  oppressive order to say just be silent  God puts you there and that's a history  for which we should lament and from  which we should repent it's a history  across the centuries of Christian  history and especially in America  because of its high ideals that should  inspire in us of vigilance that it  should not happen again we should never  say again to those who are suffering  unjustly God wants you to suffer that's  the way God made the world that's not  the God we know that's not the God we  see especially in Psalm 23 but we should  not let the historical misuse of this  scripture poison it for us because in  context we see that first Peter 2 is  about the early church those very first  Christians who in a time of persecution  when they were despised by the Roman  culture around them as atheists and  aliens how they taught each other to  conduct themselves in that world we have  to read that scripture in that context  first Peter 2 12 prior to our election  today says to all the Christians conduct  yourselves honorably amongst the  Gentiles  so that though they malign you as  evildoers because that's what Christians  were they may see your honorable deeds  and glorify God when he comes to judge  the writer of first Peter is worried  about how this despised minority is seen  by the majority and for their own safety  advises them not to cause trouble the  letter writer wants to justify to the  same Imperial and impressive order that  crucified Jesus that the Christians are  not a threat that's what first Peter 2  is about of course even historically the  author was wrong the Roman persecution  of the Christians only increased after  this was written because there's no  placating a bloodthirsty and oppressive  imperial order as we know as we see in  Syria or Libya today but there's a  difference between how these verses can  be used and that's where I want to go  today one picture I imagine is one early  Christian saying to the other keep your  head down even those of you who are  slaves don't fight back don't draw  attention to us we're in trouble that's  one scene another scene is an American  slave owner of 150 years ago saying read  your Bible you slaves slavery is a  god-ordained institution two very  different ways of looking at this verse  you have to remember that the reason  there were slaves in that early  Christian church is because they wanted  to join the Christian community because  they were excluded from all of roman  culture only the Christians welcomed the  men and even more the slaves came  because they heard in the message of  Jesus a message of liberation of human  dignity of equality and of hope even in  slaveholding America even the same  slaves who heard their masters quoting  the scripture at them  we're still drawn to a faith that was as  they heard it about freedom but how do  we know when we're misusing a scripture  like this one to justify an unjust order  and when a scripture is as Al Gore would  say an inconvenient truth that we cannot  forget when I get stuck on these  questions I have to go back to Jesus  when I worry about what to do with a  hard Scripture like this I have to go  back to Jesus and I think Jesus would  say it's what's in your heart that  matters even as you read and interpret  the scripture what's in your heart is it  fear is it love of your own privilege is  it your comfort in the given way things  are done that motivates your reading of  Scripture if so I think we're wandering  from Jesus's way can't be about fear  can't be about maintaining this order  because we know from Jesus's life that's  not what he taught that's not what he  did that's not who he spoke to I think  Jesus would say that the oppressed get  to be the interpreters of Scripture  those who are hurt most those who are  excluded most those whose rights are  forgotten they get to interpret the  scripture not their oppressors because  jesus said the first will be last and  the last shall be first if I am unjustly  imprisoned I get to say to my fellow  prisoner remember Jesus he suffered even  more than your suffering he died but he  would not lose his faith or his  principles I can say to my fellow  prisoner be comforted in your suffering  but if I'm the warden of that prison I  don't get to say remember Jesus and  except by Authority some would say  suffering can never be God's will and I  would agree if this were the kingdom of  God and God were in charge  but that's not where we are yet in a  sinful world we have to use every way we  have to survive and if that means  connecting my suffering which Jesus is  suffering and that makes it easier for  me to bear I can go with that but I  don't get to tell anyone else that  they're suffering is good or redemptive  or God's will we need to do what we need  to do to survive barbour lundblad one of  my favorite preachers in her commentary  on this passage tells a story about  Howard Thurman many of you know the  great African American author  philosopher and theologian and she  writes this about her picture of how he  might look at the scripture she tells a  story saying that he knew what it meant  to redefine himself and his children in  the face of racism she quotes his  autobiography where he recalls the day  he took his little daughters from Boston  to Daytona Beach where he'd grown up and  Thurman writes this says we sauntered  down the long street from the church to  the riverfront this had been the path of  the church procession the baptismal  ceremony in the Halifax River at length  we passed the playground at one of the  white public schools and as soon as my  daughter's olive and and saw the swings  they jumped for joy look daddy let's go  over and swing this was the inescapable  moment of truth that every black parent  in America at that time had to face what  do you say to your child at the critical  moment of this first encounter I said  you can't swing on those swings why  daddy they asked I said when we get home  and have some cold lemonade I'll tell  you so when we got home and had our  lemonade my daughter Ann had not  forgotten she pressed for the answer and  said we're home now daddy tell us about  the swings  and i said it's against the law for us  to use those swings even though it is a  public school only white children can  play there but it takes the state  legislature and the courts the sheriffs  and the policemen the white churches the  mayor's the banks and businesses the  majority of white people in the whole  state of Florida it takes all these  people to keep two little black girls  from swinging in these swings that is  how important you are I said to my  daughters never forget the estimate of  your own importance and self-worth can  be judged by how much power people are  willing to use to keep you in the place  they have assigned to you you are two  very important girls perhaps we've come  back to Psalm 23 for thou art with me  wherever I am in seasons of endurance in  seasons of revolution wherever we are  placed God is with us and turns the  powers of this world on their heads

